1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:07,800
Diwan-i-Khas is recorded as twenty-seven point four metres long and twenty point four metres wide.

2
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:14,787
The exterior ornamentation of Diwan-i-Khas is recorded as detailed pietra dura work.

3
00:00:14,787 --> 00:00:21,162
The interior ornamentation is documented as paintings decorative across the walls.

4
00:00:21,162 --> 00:00:26,862
The ceiling of Diwan-i-Khas is designed flat across the span of the hall.

5
00:00:26,862 --> 00:00:32,575
This flat ceiling is structurally supported on series of cusped arches.

6
00:00:32,575 --> 00:00:37,262
On its west side, Diwan-i-Khas featured two courtyards.

7
00:00:37,262 --> 00:00:43,200
Both of these western courtyards were enclosed by arcaded colonnades.

8
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:48,675
The Nahr-i-Bihisht channel runs directly through the centre of the hall structure.

9
00:00:48,675 --> 00:00:54,787
The combination of flat ceiling and cusped arches defines the structural framework.

10
00:00:54,787 --> 00:01:00,612
Wall paintings enrich the interior surfaces of the Hall of Private Audience.

11
00:01:00,612 --> 00:01:06,612
Pietra dura craftsmanship highlights the exterior ornamentation of the pavilion.

12
00:01:06,612 --> 00:01:13,300
The arcaded colonnades surrounding the two western courtyards provided architectural enclosure.

13
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:20,262
The central water channel of the Nahr-i-Bihisht integrates fluid movement into the stone architecture.

14
00:01:20,262 --> 00:01:27,362
Measuring twenty-seven point four by twenty point four metres, the hall displays balanced proportion.

15
00:01:27,362 --> 00:01:35,462
These structural arches, colonnades, paintings, and water channels express Mughal architectural design.

16
00:01:35,462 --> 00:01:41,737
All of these features are recorded in the historic property inventory of the Red Fort.

17
00:01:41,737 --> 00:01:47,450
Together, they define the complete architectural composition of Diwan-i-Khas.

